    "html": "<h2>Tone is a function of time, not temper</h2>\n<p>The biggest reminder mistake is letting the tone follow your frustration. A calm client at day 5 does not deserve a final notice, and a client at day 45 should not still be getting \"no rush\" emails. Map tone to days overdue and the cadence runs itself.</p>\n<h2>Friendly — 0 to 14 days overdue</h2>\n<p>Friendly assumes good intent: a missed invoice, a busy approver, a link that landed in spam. It protects the relationship and works because most late payments at this stage are simple slips.</p>\n<ul><li>Sounds like a partner, not a collector.</li><li>Short, one link, no guilt.</li><li>Goal: surface the invoice, make it easy to pay.</li></ul>\n<h2>Firm — 15 to 30 days overdue</h2>\n<p>Firm sets a boundary. The invoice is clearly late, a soft deadline is named, and any blocker is invited. Firm is specific and time-bound — not angry.</p>\n<ul><li>Names the amount and days overdue explicitly.</li><li>Proposes a settle-by date.</li><li>Goal: create a commitment, not a confrontation.</li></ul>\n<h2>Final — before any late fee or escalation</h2>\n<p>Final is the last step. It is direct, references the next action (late fee or paused work per the agreement), and stays professional. It should feel like the natural conclusion of the cadence, not a betrayal.</p>\n<ul><li>States the consequence that was agreed in advance.</li><li>Goal: close the loop before collections.</li></ul>\n<h2>How to choose in practice</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Days overdue</th><th>Tone</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0–14</td><td>Friendly</td><td>Assume a slip, protect the relationship</td></tr><tr><td>15–30</td><td>Firm</td><td>Set a boundary, name a deadline</td></tr><tr><td>31+ / pre-fee</td><td>Final</td><td>Conclude the cadence, reference the agreement</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Authoritative references</h2>\n<ul><li>Freelancers Union: https://www.freelancersunion.org/</li><li>U.S. Small Business Administration: https://www.sba.gov/</li><li>SCORE: https://www.score.org/</li></ul>\n",
    "faq": [
      {
        "question": "Should I always start friendly?",
        "answer": "Yes, for the first reminder. At 0–14 days a friendly tone assumes good intent and protects the relationship. Escalate only as the days overdue climb."
      },
      {
        "question": "When does friendly stop working?",
        "answer": "Around 15 days overdue, a friendly nudge starts to read as passive. Move to firm so the client sees a clear boundary and a deadline."
      },
      {
        "question": "Is final the same as angry?",
        "answer": "No. Final is direct and references the agreed next step (late fee or paused work), but it stays professional. Anger lowers the odds of a clean payment."
      },
      {
        "question": "Can I skip firm and go straight to final?",
        "answer": "You can, but you usually shouldn't — jumping to final too early burns the relationship and can harden resistance. Firm is the bridge."
      },
      {
        "question": "Does Dunner pick the tone for me?",
        "answer": "You pick the tone (friendly / firm / final); Dunner writes that version from your client, amount, and days overdue. You stay in control of the cadence."
      }
    ],
